ricepilot doctor: no problems, 1 hazard. nothing was changed; the commands are for you to run.

HAZARD 1 of 1: /home
  may not be snapshotted.
  rule: NOT-POSSIBLE.md#snapshotting

  doctor could not read `home` — snapper's configs are often root-only — so
  /home may be covered after all; `snapper list-configs` says.

  everything ricepilot moves lives under /home. unless one of those configs
  covers it, nothing there is covered by a snapshot.

  ricepilot cannot take one: a snapshot needs root, and ricepilot never
  runs sudo. on a btrfs /home this is the command, as root:

  run:
    snapper -c home create-config /home

not checked, or worth knowing:
  - profile `new`: <HOME>/rice/new/hypr/hyprland.lua is a Lua config, which nothing can check before a login (NOT-POSSIBLE.md#verify-lua-config)
  - profile `old`: <HOME>/rice/old/hypr/hyprland.lua is a Lua config, which nothing can check before a login (NOT-POSSIBLE.md#verify-lua-config)
  - profile `old`: no manifest has been recorded for it yet, so its tree was not compared with anything

ok: no interrupted operation; 3 owned links intact; the ledger and the manifests agree; 2 hypr trees have an entry file; 1 profile tree matches its recorded manifest; rescue.sh restores generation 0000 and parses; attic: 1 entry, 0 B (the newest, <TS>, 0 B); no verify-config copies; no interrupted gc; no caelestia theme daemon running
